{
  peppy_schema: "pairing/v1",
  manifest: {
    name: "drive_link",
    tag: "v1",
    description: [
      "The exclusive control conversation between one leader instance and one mobile base, as a pairing: the leader streams the velocity it wants, and the follower streams back the velocity the base executes, where its odometry places it, and whether it follows the leader, each direction scoped to the pair.",
      "Velocity conventions, identical in both directions: a velocity is a twist of the robot frame, base_state's base_footprint, as base_state defines the base's twist: in the robot frame, linear_velocity is x forward, y leftward and z up in m/s, with a z of 0, and angular_velocity turns about +z alone in rad/s, counterclockwise seen from above. drive_states gives the base's odometry as base_state's base_states does, in odom, and the limits of its motion as base_state's describe_base gives them, from which a leader lays out and scales the velocities it sends. base_state also defines the odometry frame, the modes, silence, rest and the limiter that this pairing names. Timestamps are on the clock of transforms, which the follower's instance reads, the simulation clock in a simulation. The follower places the velocity of each setpoint in the robot frame by its tree at the setpoint's timestamp, or at the latest time for a setpoint with no timestamp, as transforms defines a lookup. A setpoint in the robot frame stands in it at any time, as transforms defines a frame looked up in itself; a leader whose clock is not the clock of transforms gives a setpoint in another frame no timestamp. Every 0.5 s rule of this pairing counts from the arrival of setpoints on that clock.",
      "The leader streams from each setpoint that arrives until more than 0.5 s pass with no setpoint. While the leader streams and no hold keeps the base from it, the follower commands the velocity of the newest setpoint, placed in the robot frame, through base_state's limiter; when the stream ends, the follower brings the base to rest. A leader therefore sends a setpoint more often than every 0.5 s while it wants motion, and setpoints of zero velocity to bring the base to rest sooner. A setpoint with a value that is not finite, one that the follower cannot place by its tree, and one whose velocity, placed in the robot frame, has a linear_velocity whose z, or an angular_velocity whose x or y, lies farther than 1e-9 from 0 count as no setpoint: the follower drops each.",
      "Whoever has the base keeps it. Four holds keep the base from the leader, and the follower drops each setpoint that arrives during one. A mode other than standing and moving holds the base, its command 0, and so does a silence of the base's motion sensing, the follower bringing the base to rest; when the leader streams as either starts, the hold lasts on after it ends until more than 0.5 s pass with no setpoint. A robot stop, the stop of robot_stop on the follower's node, brings the base to rest and holds it until more than 0.5 s pass, from the stop on, with no setpoint. A goal of the follower's node holds the base while it runs when it moves the base, a joint that the base's own motion also commands, such as an arm joint that a walking controller drives, or a part that the robot moves only while its base rests, such as a lift, which the node's description names. Such a goal is refused while the leader streams, with the failure kind the goal's contract states, and a goal that moves a part of the last kind is also refused with `precondition_failed` while the base moves, as base_state defines it. A setpoint that arrives during a hold counts for every 0.5 s rule. A node that leads the base and follows a leader of its own on another pair of this pairing, such as a navigator that a joystick node leads, holds that leader through a robot stop: from the first drive_states of the base that reports robot_stop, it drops that leader's setpoints and reports robot_stop to it, until more than 0.5 s pass with no setpoint of that leader, counted from the first drive_states of the base that reports another follow_state.",
    ],
  },
  roles: ["leader", "follower"],
  topics: [
    {
      emitted_by: "leader",
      name: "drive_setpoints",
      description: "The velocity of the robot frame that the leader wants the base to drive at, a twist given in frame at timestamp.",
      // High-frequency stream where only the latest setpoint matters;
      // occasional drops are acceptable.
      qos_profile: "sensor_data",
      message_format: {
        frame: { $type: "string", $optional: true, $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames; absent, the robot frame." },
        timestamp: { $type: "time", $optional: true, $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold. A command leaves it out for the latest time; a report leaves it out for values that hold at every time." },
        linear_velocity: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m/s)." },
        angular_velocity: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "About x, y, z (rad/s)." },
      },
    },
    {
      emitted_by: "follower",
      name: "drive_states",
      description: "The velocity that the base executes and where its odometry places the robot frame, as base_state's base_states carries them and when it publishes them, the limits of the base's motion, as base_state's describe_base gives them, and whether the follower follows the leader.",
      // High-frequency stream where only the latest state matters;
      // occasional drops are acceptable.
      qos_profile: "sensor_data",
      message_format: {
        timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold." },
        odometry_start_timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "When the odometry frame was fixed: a new value means the odometry started over at zero." },
        frame: { $type: "string", $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames; the record's twist is in child_frame." },
        child_frame: { $type: "string", $description: "The frame whose pose in frame the record gives, as transforms names frames." },
        pose: {
          $type: "object",
          $description: "The robot frame's pose in odom, as base_state's base_states carries it.",
          position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
          orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]." },
        },
        twist: {
          $type: "object",
          $description: "The velocity the base executes in the robot frame, as base_state's base_states carries it.",
          linear_velocity: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m/s)." },
          angular_velocity: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "About x, y, z (rad/s)." },
        },
        mode: { $type: "string", $enum: ["off", "damped", "posed", "transition", "upright", "standing", "moving", "fallen"], $description: "What the base can do at timestamp, as base_state's base_states carries it." },
        parts_within_footprint: { $type: "bool", $description: "Whether every limb stands within the footprint at timestamp, as base_state's base_states carries it." },
        linear_limits: {
          $type: "object",
          $description: "The limits of the base's linear velocity in the robot frame, each an x, y pair, as base_state's describe_base gives them.",
          min_velocity: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 2, $description: "The most negative velocity the base executes along x and along y (m/s), each 0 or below: backward and rightward." },
          max_velocity: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 2, $description: "The most positive velocity the base executes along x and along y (m/s), each 0 or above: forward and leftward." },
          max_acceleration: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 2, $description: "Along x and along y, the fastest the magnitude of the velocity may grow (m/s^2)." },
          max_deceleration: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 2, $description: "Along x and along y, the fastest the magnitude of the velocity may fall (m/s^2)." },
          deadband: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 2, $description: "Along x and along y, the deadband of the axis (m/s)." },
          rest_velocity: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 2, $description: "Along x and along y, the largest magnitude of the twist's linear_velocity at which the base counts as at rest (m/s)." },
        },
        angular_limits: {
          $type: "object",
          $description: "The limits of the base's turn rate in the robot frame, counterclockwise seen from above, as base_state's describe_base gives them.",
          min_rate: { $type: "f64", $description: "The most negative turn rate the base executes (rad/s), 0 or below: clockwise." },
          max_rate: { $type: "f64", $description: "The most positive turn rate the base executes (rad/s), 0 or above: counterclockwise." },
          max_acceleration: { $type: "f64", $description: "The fastest the magnitude of the turn rate may grow (rad/s^2)." },
          max_deceleration: { $type: "f64", $description: "The fastest the magnitude of the turn rate may fall (rad/s^2)." },
          deadband: { $type: "f64", $description: "The deadband of the turn rate (rad/s)." },
          rest_rate: { $type: "f64", $description: "The largest magnitude of the z of the twist's angular_velocity at which the base counts as at rest (rad/s)." },
        },
        follow_state: { $type: "string", $enum: ["following", "base_mode", "base_silence", "robot_stop", "follower_goal"], $description: "Whether the follower follows the leader, the first of these that holds: base_mode while the mode holds the base; base_silence while a silence holds the base; robot_stop while a robot stop holds the base; follower_goal while a goal of the follower's node holds the base; following otherwise, when the follower follows the leader or would follow its next setpoint." },
      },
    },
  ],
}
